The NHS states that iron is essential for the production of haemoglobin, which transports oxygen in the blood (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/iron-deficiency-anemia/). Dietary sources of iron include red meat, liver, kidney, beans and fortified cereals (https://www.nhs.uk/nutrition/food-and-nutrition/iron/). The recommended intake for adult men is 8.7 mg per day and for adult women 18 mg per day (https://www.nhs.uk/nutrition/food-and-nutrition/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-and-mineral-supplements/iron/). The upper tolerable intake level for adults is 45 mg per day (https://www.nhs.uk/nutrition/food-and-nutrition/vitamin-and-mineral-supplements/iron/). NHS guidance notes that iron supplements are available on prescription for iron deficiency anaemia (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/iron-deficiency-anemia/treatment/).

