The available sources state that vitamin K is essential for the activation of blood coagulation factors and plays an important role in the normal coagulation pathway (https://www.hra.nhs.uk/planning-and-improving-research/application-summaries/research-summaries/vitamin-k-deficiency-in-patients-undergoing-hip-surgery). It is found in green leafy vegetables such as broccoli and spinach, vegetable oils, and cereal grains (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k), as well as in plant oils such as soya, corn and olive oil, nuts and seeds, fortified breakfast cereals, and also in small amounts in meat and dairy foods (https://www.nhslanarkshire.scot.nhs.uk/patient-information-leaflets/nutrition-dietetics/pil-fodsrc-21-12986-l). No UK source provides a reference intake for vitamin K (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k). The recommended upper limit for supplemental vitamin K is 1 mg per day, which is unlikely to cause any harm (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k). The sources do not contain advice about stopping warfarin; you should discuss any changes to medication with a healthcare professional.

