Vitamin K is essential for the formation of clotting factors that enable blood to clot when vessels are injured, as described by NHS pathology services (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/vitamin-k-profile-vit-k-pivka11). It also plays a role in bone health and is a natural substance that helps blood clot properly, according to NHS patient information (https://www.ulh.nhs.uk/patients/patient-information-library/vitamin-k-and-your-new-baby). The recommended intake is about 1 microgram per kilogram of body weight each day, based on NHS guidance (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k). Dietary sources include green leafy vegetables such as broccoli and spinach, vegetable oils, and fortified breakfast cereals (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k; https://www.nhslanarkshire.scot.nhs.uk/patient-information-leaflets/nutrition-dietetics/pil-fodsrc-21-12986-l). Vitamin K is also used as an antidote to coumarin‑type anticoagulant overdosage, reflecting its regulatory status (https://www.ncl-jointformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09.06.06&SubSectionID=A100).

