Iodine is a key part of thyroid hormones needed for growth, metabolism and early brain development (https://library.sheffieldchildrens.nhs.uk/iodine). It helps make thyroid hormones that maintain healthy cells and metabolic rate (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iodine). Good food sources include cows' milk, dairy products, eggs, sea fish and shellfish (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iodine). Plant foods such as cereals and grains may also contain iodine, though levels vary with soil content (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iodine). The recommended daily intake for adults is 140 micrograms (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iodine). The upper safe limit from supplements is 0.5 mg per day (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iodine). Iodine (Lugol's Iodine) oral solution is listed in the British National Formulary (https://www.northeastnorthcumbriaformulary.nhs.uk/searchresults.asp?SearchVar=Iodine)  

